      ],
      "topic": [],
      "status": 200,
      "revision": "b2be39ff25251890830c98fdb9085b4d7c6ba7cc",
      "reply": {
        "answer": "Radar is a major threat to aircraft on a modern battlefield. A radar measures range by timing its pulses and measuring closure via Doppler shift. Pulse-Doppler radars can remove ground returns by rejecting objects with low velocity relative to the ground; however, this creates a weakness known as the Doppler notch, where a target flying at a 90 degree aspect (straight across the radar's line of sight) can be hidden in the clutter because it has no speed toward or away from the radar. \n\nA radar warning receiver (RWR) is an electronic warfare support system that uses antennas to feed a processor, which matches signals against an emitter identification table to display symbols and tones on a scope. The RWR provides information regarding the threat system type, the radar's bearing relative to the aircraft's nose, and the radar mode (such as search, track, or missile launch). It is noted that a receiver should be treated as a direction finder rather than a precise position indicator. Specific brevity terms for tracking include a spike for an airborne radar and MUD for a surface radar, while no indications are referred to as NAKED.",
        "ids": [
          "/manual/abnormal-operations/threat-avoidance/#radar",
          "/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#rwr",
          "/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/",
          "/manual/sensors-situational-awareness/radar-and-warning/#notch"
        ],
        "topic": [],
        "references": [
          {
            "id": "/manual/abnormal-operations/threat-avoidance/#radar",
            "title": "Threat Avoidance and Defeat \u00b7 Defeating a radar-guided SAM"
